# Livewire security conventions

Every Livewire interaction is an HTTP request; the client controls everything it
can see. Treat public properties and public methods as untrusted, public
endpoints.

- Validate public properties with `#[Validate]` (or `rules()`) and call `$this->validate()` before persisting.
- Mark server-only context (IDs, flags) `#[Locked]`; never assign untrusted request data into a locked property.
- Authorize every public action server-side with `$this->authorize('ability', $model)`, inside the method.
- Never trust action parameters: resolve the model from the parameter, then authorize it against the current user.
- Keep internal helpers `protected`/`private` — only front-end-called methods are `public`.
- Persist custom auth middleware via `Livewire::addPersistentMiddleware([...])`.
- Uploads: `WithFileUploads`, validate type + size, let `store()` name the file; never `getClientOriginalName()` on a public disk.
- Keep secrets out of public properties (they ship to the browser); use `protected` / `#[Computed]`.
- Rate-limit sensitive actions with Laravel's `RateLimiter`.
- Stay patched: keep `livewire/livewire` current, run `composer audit`. Livewire 4 is not a security overhaul — same threat model as v3.
- Write tests first (per `tdd`), including an unauthorized-call test and a tamper test for each action.

Full policy: `ai/skills/livewire/livewire-policy.md`. Full guidance: `ai/skills/livewire/SKILL.md`.
